        <div class="content lesson-content" style="font-size: 1.05rem; line-height: 1.7; color: var(--text-secondary);">
            <p>The <strong>Spanning Tree Protocol (STP)</strong> is a critical Layer 2 network protocol designed to prevent loops in Ethernet networks that have redundant links.</p>
            
            <h3>The Danger of Layer 2 Loops</h3>
            <p>Redundancy is good for reliability, but without STP, plugging switches into each other in a loop is disastrous. Ethernet frames do not have a "Time to Live" (TTL) mechanism like IP packets. If a loop exists:</p>
            <ul>
                <li><strong>Broadcast Storms:</strong> A single broadcast frame will circulate infinitely, multiplying and completely overwhelming network bandwidth and CPU resources.</li>
                <li><strong>Duplicate Frames:</strong> End devices will receive the exact same frame multiple times.</li>
                <li><strong>MAC Table Instability:</strong> Switches will constantly see the same MAC address arriving on different ports, causing the MAC address table to rapidly flap and break connectivity.</li>
            </ul>

            <h3>How STP Works Conceptually</h3>
            <p>STP mathematically transforms a physical loop into a logical tree topology by systematically blocking certain ports. The core concepts are:</p>
            
            <ul>
                <li><strong>Root Bridge:</strong> One switch is elected as the "king" of the network (based on the lowest Bridge ID). All other switches map their shortest path to this Root Bridge.</li>
                <li><strong>Bridge ID:</strong> A combination of a priority value and the switch's MAC address used during the election process.</li>
                <li><strong>Path Cost:</strong> A value assigned to each link (usually based on link speed) to determine the best route to the Root Bridge.</li>
                <li><strong>Forwarding Path:</strong> The best paths that are kept active to pass traffic.</li>
                <li><strong>Blocking / Discarding Path:</strong> Redundant links are put into a state where they listen for STP messages but do not forward regular data traffic. If the main link fails, STP will unblock the redundant link to restore connectivity.</li>
            </ul>

            <h3>A Simple Topology Example</h3>
            <pre style="background: transparent; border: none; padding: 0;">
      Switch A (Root Bridge)
       ↙       ↘
Switch B &mdash;&mdash;&mdash;&mdash;&mdash; Switch C
            </pre>
            <p>In this triangle topology, there is a physical loop. STP will elect <strong>Switch A</strong> as the Root Bridge. Switch B and Switch C both have direct links to the Root. To break the loop, STP will evaluate the link between B and C. One side of that link will be placed into a blocking state (preventing forwarding simultaneously under normal operation), effectively breaking the loop while keeping a backup path available.</p>

            <h3>Modern Variants: RSTP</h3>
            <p>While the original STP (802.1D) could take up to 50 seconds to converge after a topology change, modern networks use <strong>Rapid Spanning Tree Protocol (RSTP, 802.1w)</strong>. RSTP operates on the same core concepts but can recover from failures in just a few milliseconds. It introduces newer terminology (like the Discarding state instead of Blocking/Listening), but the conceptual goal—loop prevention—remains identical.</p>
        </div>
        
